{"id":"leetcode-351","version":1,"status":"approved","title":"Android Unlock Patterns","summary":"Android devices feature a 3x3 grid of dots for setting an unlock pattern.","statement":"Android devices feature a 3x3 grid of dots for setting an unlock pattern. A valid pattern connects dots in a sequence where each dot is distinct, and any line segment connecting two consecutive dots must pass through any intermediate dot that appears in the sequence before. Count the number of unique valid patterns using at least m keys and at most n keys.","constraints":["1 <= m, n <= 9. Count ordered patterns whose length is at least m and at most n; if m > n, return 0.","Number the board row by row: 1 2 3 / 4 5 6 / 7 8 9. A dot may appear only once.","A move through the exact center of another dot is allowed only if that dot was selected earlier. For example 1->3 needs 2; 2->9 has no intermediate dot and is allowed."],"topics":["recursion","backtracking","dynamic-programming","bitmask"],"prerequisites":[],"signature":{"name":"count_unlock_patterns","parameters":[{"name":"m","annotation":"int","adapter":"raw","source_parameter":null},{"name":"n","annotation":"int","adapter":"raw","source_parameter":null}],"returns":"int","return_adapter":"raw","node_policy":"values","result_mode":"return","mutated_parameter":null,"immutable_parameters":[]},"starter_code":"def count_unlock_patterns(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    # Write your solution here.\n    pass\n","reference_solution":"def count_unlock_patterns(m, n):\n    from functools import lru_cache\n    if m > n:\n        return 0\n    jumps = {}\n    for a, b, middle in ((1,3,2),(1,7,4),(1,9,5),(2,8,5),\n                          (3,7,5),(3,9,6),(4,6,5),(7,9,8)):\n        jumps[a,b] = jumps[b,a] = middle\n    @lru_cache(None)\n    def visit(last, mask):\n        length = mask.bit_count()\n        result = int(m <= length <= n)\n        if length == n:\n            return result\n        for nxt in range(1,10):\n            middle = jumps.get((last,nxt))\n            if not mask & (1 << nxt) and (middle is None or mask & (1 << middle)):\n                result += visit(nxt,mask | (1 << nxt))\n        return result\n    return sum(visit(start,1 << start) for start in range(1,10))\n","brute_force_solution":null,"known_wrong_solutions":["def count_unlock_patterns(m, n):\n    from math import perm\n    return sum(perm(9,k) for k in range(m,n+1))","def count_unlock_patterns(m, n):\n    from functools import lru_cache\n    if m > n:\n        return 0\n    jumps = {}\n    for a, b, middle in ((1,3,2),(1,7,4),(1,9,5),(2,8,5),\n                          (3,7,5),(3,9,6),(4,6,5),(7,9,8)):\n        jumps[a,b] = jumps[b,a] = middle\n    @lru_cache(None)\n    def visit(last, mask):\n        length = mask.bit_count()\n        result = int(m <= length <= n)\n        if length == n:\n            return result\n        for nxt in range(1,10):\n            middle = jumps.get((last,nxt))\n            if not mask & (1 << nxt) and middle is None:\n                result += visit(nxt,mask | (1 << nxt))\n        return result\n    return sum(visit(start,1 << start) for start in range(1,10))\n"],"target_time_complexity":"O(K^2 * 2^K), K = 9","target_space_complexity":"O(K * 2^K)","estimated_minutes":35,"rubric":{"algorithmic_insight":2,"prerequisites":1,"implementation":1,"performance_pressure":1,"edge_cases":1},"hints":["Whether a jump is legal depends on the set of previously selected dots.","Recurse on the last dot and a visited bitmask. Memoize these states.","Count the current state if its size is within [m,n]; extend to each unused dot when its required midpoint is already visited.","Precompute the eight unordered midpoint-crossing pairs. Memoized search counts each ordered path while sharing suffix states."],"editorial":"A state consists of the last selected dot and the visited set. Every valid next dot is unused and either has no midpoint on its segment or crosses a previously visited midpoint. Count each state at a permitted length, then extend until n dots. Summing over all starts counts ordered patterns. The independent oracle exhausts every dot permutation and checks geometric interior points; all 81 parameter pairs are deterministic tests.","tests":[{"id":"lc-351-1","arguments":[1,1],"expected":9,"comparator":"exact","visible":true,"explanation":"Each of the nine individual dots forms one pattern, so the count is 9.","expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-2","arguments":[1,2],"expected":65,"comparator":"exact","visible":true,"explanation":"There are 9 one-dot patterns and 56 legal ordered two-dot patterns, totaling 65. An unvisited midpoint blocks jumps such as 1->3.","expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-3","arguments":[1,3],"expected":385,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-4","arguments":[1,4],"expected":2009,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-5","arguments":[1,5],"expected":9161,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-6","arguments":[1,6],"expected":35177,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-7","arguments":[1,7],"expected":108089,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-8","arguments":[1,8],"expected":248793,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-9","arguments":[1,9],"expected":389497,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-10","arguments":[2,1],"expected":0,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-11","arguments":[2,2],"expected":56,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-351-12","arguments":[2,3],"expected":376,"comparator":"exact","visible":